  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "short": "Tables",
      "title": "Data in tables",
      "chapter": "setup",
      "body": "Most apps start with a [[relational]] (SQL) database such as PostgreSQL or MySQL. Data lives in tables with a fixed set of columns — a [[schema]].\n\nTables point at each other with keys, and a **join** combines them in one query: “every order with its customer’s name”.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "SQL databases have decades of tooling behind them. When unsure, they’re a very safe default."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "joins": "yes",
        "tx": "yes",
        "schema": "fixed",
        "scaleOut": "hard"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "short": "Transactions",
      "title": "All or nothing: transactions",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "Moving $50 from Ana to Ben is two updates. If the server crashes between them, money disappears.\n\nA [[transaction]] groups them: both happen, or neither does. Relational databases guarantee this (the **ACID** properties), which is why they hold payments, orders and inventory.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "ACID: Atomic (all or nothing), Consistent (rules hold), Isolated (no half-done views), Durable (committed means saved)."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Two writes, one transaction"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "joins": "yes",
        "tx": "yes",
        "schema": "fixed",
        "scaleOut": "hard"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "short": "Growing pains",
      "title": "Growing pains",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "Your product catalog lands in the same database. Shoes have sizes, laptops have CPUs, books have authors — so the products table sprouts hundreds of mostly-empty columns, and every new product type means another migration on a 400-million-row table. Some of those rewrite or lock the whole table for hours.\n\nMeanwhile all writes still go through one database server.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warning",
        "text": "Rigid schemas and a single write server are the two classic pain points of relational databases at scale."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Product catalog in the SQL database",
        "~ Products table: 300 columns, mostly empty"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "joins": "yes",
        "tx": "yes",
        "schema": "fixed",
        "scaleOut": "hard"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "short": "Documents",
      "title": "Documents: each record its own shape",
      "chapter": "solution",
      "body": "A [[document store]] (such as MongoDB) keeps each record as a self-contained document. A shoe and a laptop can have completely different fields, with no migration to run — though your code must now cope with every shape it has ever written.\n\nEverything about one product lives together, so reading it is a single fetch, and documents spread naturally across many servers.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Documents shine when you read and write whole objects at once — catalogs, profiles, content."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Document store for the catalog",
        "~ SQL database back to orders & users"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "joins": "limited",
        "tx": "per doc",
        "schema": "flexible",
        "scaleOut": "easy"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "short": "Key–value",
      "title": "Key–value: just look it up",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "The simplest store of all: a [[key–value]] store (Redis, DynamoDB) maps a key to a value. No queries, no joins — get and set by key, in about a millisecond.\n\nBecause each key stands alone, the data splits across many nodes almost for free.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Perfect for sessions, carts, feature flags and counters — anything you always fetch by one id."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Key–value cluster for sessions"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "joins": "no",
        "tx": "limited",
        "schema": "none",
        "scaleOut": "easy"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s6",
      "short": "Trade-offs",
      "title": "What NoSQL gives up",
      "chapter": "tradeoff",
      "body": "NoSQL stores scale out easily largely *because* they give things up: joins across records, multi-record transactions, and often strict consistency.\n\n• Data shape — SQL: Fixed tables; NoSQL: Flexible\n• Joins — SQL: Yes; NoSQL: In your code\n• Transactions — SQL: Across rows; NoSQL: Usually one record\n• Scaling writes — SQL: Hard; NoSQL: Built in\n\nThese are defaults, not laws, and the lines keep blurring: PostgreSQL stores JSON documents, MongoDB and DynamoDB offer multi-record transactions, and distributed SQL databases (Spanner, CockroachDB) scale writes out while keeping joins and transactions — paying for it in latency and complexity.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warning",
        "text": "Picking NoSQL “for scale” you don’t have yet often means rebuilding joins and transactions by hand."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "joins": "limited",
        "tx": "limited",
        "schema": "flexible",
        "scaleOut": "easy"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s7",
      "short": "Use both",
      "title": "Use each where it fits",
      "chapter": "solution",
      "body": "Real systems mix them — [[polyglot persistence]]. Money and orders stay relational for transactions; the catalog lives in documents; sessions go to a key–value store.\n\nStart with one database, and add another only when a kind of data clearly wants it.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "The question isn’t “SQL or NoSQL?” but “what does this data need: relations, flexibility, or raw lookup speed?”"
      },
      "changes": [
        "~ Orders & payments → SQL",
        "~ Catalog → documents",
        "~ Sessions → key–value"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "joins": "yes",
        "tx": "yes",
        "schema": "flexible",
        "scaleOut": "easy"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "as-5": {
          "visible": false
        },
