    {
      "id": "s8",
      "short": "Search",
      "title": "Case: “red running shoes”",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "Shoppers type free text into a search box. In SQL that becomes `WHERE name LIKE '%red%'` on millions of rows — slow, and it can’t rank results, handle typos or match “shoe” to “shoes”.\n\nA [[search engine]] (Elasticsearch, OpenSearch) builds an inverted index: for every word, the list of documents containing it. It’s fed from the main database by a stream of changes.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "The search index is a copy, not the source of truth — it can always be rebuilt from the main database."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Search engine for the catalog",
        "+ Change stream: SQL → search index"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "joins": "yes",
        "tx": "yes",
        "schema": "flexible",
        "scaleOut": "easy"
      },
      "overrides": {
